<Layout title="The decisions behind our modelling approach" description="The normative ODR-0071 category subset and supporting modelling decisions, relevant OPDA ADRs, implementation boundaries and reconciliation with earlier PDTF explanations.">
  <h1>The decisions behind our modelling approach</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Our modelling method is governed by the relevant ontology decision records in
    <em>semantic-modelling</em>. ODR-0071 supplies the category framework; OPDA selects
    the eight concerns needed for this programme and applies the supporting decisions
    within that scope. These are modelling rules, not an optional reading list.
  </p>

  <h2 id="authority">A required method and an implemented model are different facts</h2>
  <p>
    An ontology decision record (ODR) explains a modelling choice and its consequences.
    An architecture decision record (ADR) records how OPDA applies the approach to its
    programme and website. The selected source ODRs are normative for how we model;
    individual definitions, mappings and candidate packages still need evidence and
    human review. A normative method does not turn a generated candidate into an
    approved standard.
  </p>
  <p>
    The source-method ODR numbers and OPDA ODR numbers are separate registers. For
    example, source-method ODR-0025 describes role views, while
    <a href="/modelling/odr/odr-0025">OPDA ODR-0025</a> describes the earlier
    schema-derived implementation's entailment regime. This guide names the register
    explicitly so matching numbers are not mistaken for the same decision.
  </p>

  <h2 id="source-odr-0071">ODR-0071: select the modelling concerns deliberately</h2>
  <p>
    Source-method ODR-0071, <cite>Ontology Modelling Category Framework</cite>, is
    Accepted, originally dated 2 April 2026, with later amendments establishing
    fourteen categories. OPDA does not adopt all fourteen. The selection already
    recorded in <a href="/modelling/adr/adr-0063">ADR-0063</a> and
    <a href="/modelling/adr/adr-0067">ADR-0067</a> retains the original category
    numbers, rather than renumbering the subset into a different framework.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>The eight normative concerns selected for OPDA</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Source category</th><th scope="col">Supporting record</th><th scope="col">What we model</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">1 — Domain structure</th><td>ODR-0071a</td><td>Things, identity, attributes, roles and relationships within a context.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">2 — Vocabulary and taxonomy</th><td>ODR-0071b</td><td>Governed concepts, values, labels and broader/narrower organisation.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">5 — Classification metadata</th><td>ODR-0071e</td><td>Independent ways to organise and find model content without inventing inheritance.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">7 — Validation and constraints</th><td>ODR-0071g</td><td>Explicit, testable requirements for the declared data and use.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">8 — Cross-domain mappings</th><td>ODR-0071h</td><td>Strategic context relationships and separately qualified term correspondences.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">9 — Provenance and quality</th><td>ODR-0071i</td><td>Sources, attribution, derivation and evidence for assessing quality.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">10 — Temporal state and history</th><td>ODR-0071j</td><td>When facts apply, when they were recorded, and how states and versions change.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">11 — Access control and data sensitivity</th><td>ODR-0071k</td><td>Meaning associated with sensitivity, purpose, consent and access responsibilities.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Categories 3 (process modelling), 4 (service architecture), 6 (governance and
    compliance as an ontology), 12 (capability and intent), 13 (source mapping) and
    14 (data products) are outside the required OPDA ontology scope. This does not
    remove programme governance or historical schema-mapping evidence. It means
    we are not building those additional models as part of this section's mandate.
    The <a href="/semantic-modelling/coverage">coverage guide</a> explains how to
    apply the retained concerns without creating unnecessary artefacts.
  </p>

  <h2 id="supporting-odrs">Supporting source ODRs: the rules behind the choices</h2>
  <p>
    The following records are read with their later amendments. Older text retained
    inside a decision is not an alternative current rule when an explicit amendment
    replaces it. Source paths are relative to the <em>semantic-modelling</em>
    decision register; the explanations here use property examples and OPDA terminology.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Normative source-method rules applied within the OPDA category subset</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Records</th><th scope="col">Required modelling discipline</th><th scope="col">Read the explanation</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">ODR-0015, ODR-0040, ODR-0106</th><td>Boundaries follow meaning and authority. Separate semantic ownership, subject classification, organisational accountability and system topology.</td><td><a href="/semantic-modelling/bounded-contexts">Contexts and semantic homes</a></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">ODR-0010, ODR-0016, ODR-0023</th><td>Keep classification separate from inheritance; use SKOS for governed vocabularies and reviewed conceptual correspondences, including class IRIs. Distinguish a closed choice list from an open population of resources.</td><td><a href="/semantic-modelling/linked-data-languages">Linked-data languages</a></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">ODR-0025, ODR-0026</th><td>Distinguish enduring kinds, contextual roles and lifecycle phases. Put properties on the type or role that introduces their meaning.</td><td><a href="/semantic-modelling/identity-roles-and-phases">Identity, roles and phases</a></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">ODR-0087, ODR-0096, ODR-0098</th><td>Compare identity criteria rather than label similarity; preserve qualified mappings with SSSOM/SEMAPV justification and source versions.</td><td><a href="/semantic-modelling/evidence-and-mappings">Evidence and mappings</a></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">ODR-0112, ODR-0115, ODR-0118</th><td>Use concern categories to organise construction and governance, with foundational and OntoClean analysis across them; do not inherit domain classes from external upper ontologies.</td><td><a href="/semantic-modelling/modelling-rules#upper">Foundational analysis</a></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">ODR-0086, ODR-0089</th><td>Apply scoped Dublin Core metadata reuse and conditional ODRL through its DPV profile, without importing unrelated ontology categories or claiming runtime enforcement.</td><td><a href="/semantic-modelling/standards">Concern-specific standards</a></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">ODR-0014, ODR-0030, ODR-0036</th><td>Preserve language semantics; distinguish documentary annotations, the declared inference subset and validation rules.</td><td><a href="#semantics">The domain/range correction</a></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">ODR-0127</th><td>Record strategic DDD context-map relationships separately from term mappings and connect them to the individual mappings that apply.</td><td><a href="/semantic-modelling/context-maps">Context maps</a></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    These decisions do not import the source project's business content, deployment
    architecture or implementation tooling. The selected rules govern OPDA modelling;
    term-level use, candidate conformance and release status must be demonstrated
    in the OPDA artefacts themselves.
  </p>

